Hooks of a release: ota/hooks/<version>.sh or <version>-<name>.sh go into the
package that updates to <version> and run as root after its files are in
place (OTA_FROM, OTA_TO, OTA_DUALBOOT=0|1 in the environment). For what a file
copy cannot do: a group to add, a service to enable, a setting to change in a
file the user may have edited. A hook must be safe to run twice. A hook that
fails undoes the whole update.

The clients of the 1.1 test builds run hooks with the PATH of whoever ran the
client, and sudo keeps ark's, which has no sbin: a hook sets its own PATH.

A hook that changes files adds their paths to /var/lib/darkos-ota/merged, one
per line, and the client (1.1 on) puts them back from the snapshot on a
rollback.

all-<name>.sh goes into every package, after the release's own hooks, in name
order:
- all-fix-root-dir.sh gives / back to root (the images up to 1.1's test
  builds had it as ark's) and removes what PPSSPP 1.12.3 left there;
- all-keep-quickmode.sh, while Quick Mode is on, puts its pause.sh and
  finish.sh back over the update's, and its entry in Options -> Advanced;
- all-ldconfig.sh rebuilds the dynamic linker's cache, which packages do not
  carry;
- all-merge-dpkg.sh merges the package database (dpkg status, apt's
  extended_states) with the device's, from the old build's copy the package
  carries in hooks/base/. It comes last, and never fails.
